namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TopicResizing Resizing, being a Treatise on the Enlargement and Contraction of Matrices

\eigenAutoToc

\section TopicResizing_Resize Resizing, in its Plain and Unvarnished Form, with \link PlainObjectBase::resize(Index,Index) resize() \endlink

The most basic method to change the size of matrices or vectors is \link PlainObjectBase::resize(Index,Index) resize(rows, cols) \endlink, which is to a Matrix what the removal to a new house is to a family of settled habits. 
It takes the new number of rows and columns as arguments, and asks no questions.

\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
m << 1, 2, 3, 4;
m.resize(3,3);
// m is now 3x3. 
// OLD values are lost. NEW values are uninitialized.
\endcode

The \c resize() method is **destructive** if the total number of coefficients (rows  x columns) differs from the previous one; it removes the household without troubling itself about the furniture. 
Meaning that all previous values are lost and the newly allocated coefficients are **uninitialized**, which is to say that the new apartments are occupied by whatever the previous tenants of that corner of the Heap may have chanced to leave in them. 
You should fill them before use.

\subsection TopicResizing_ResizeNoOp The special case of "No-Op" resizing, in which Nothing Whatever Is Done, and Yet a Great Deal Appears to Have Happened
If you resize a matrix while keeping the total number of coefficients unchanged, the existing values are preserved in memory; the inhabitants, that is, are not disturbed in their beds. 
(Meaning when old_rows x old_cols = new_rows x new_cols)

However, because Eigen stores matrices in **column-major** order by default, the logical position of these values may change; the lodgers remain, to a man, in the rooms they had before, but the street has been renumbered about them.

\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
m << 1, 2, 3, 4; 
// m is now:    1 2
//              3 4    
// Memory storage: [1, 3, 2, 4]

// Resizing to 1x4 (total size 4 is unchanged)
m.resize(1,4); 
// m is now:   1 3 2 4
// The memory [1, 3, 2, 4] was not touched, but is now interpreted as a 1x4 matrix. 
\endcode

\subsection TopicResizing_ResizeNoChange Resizing only one dimension, the other being Left in Perfect Tranquillity

To resize only one dimension while leaving the other unchanged, 
you can pass \c Eigen::NoChange as the parameter for the dimension you wish to keep; a form of words which signifies, in the Library, what "no alteration is proposed in this quarter" signifies in a Parliamentary bill.

\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
// Resize rows to 5, keep columns at 2
m.resize(5, Eigen::NoChange); 

// Resize columns to 3, keep rows at 5
m.resize(Eigen::NoChange, 3);
\endcode

\subsection TopicResizing_ResizeVectors Resizing vectors

Resizing for vectors behaves the same way as for matrices, a vector being merely a matrix of a retiring disposition, who has but a single column or a single row to his name. 
You provide the new size as an argument to \c resize().

\code
VectorXd v(3);
v << 1, 2, 3;
v.resize(5);   
// v is now of size 5 and the values are uninitialized.
\endcode

\subsection TopicResizing_ResizeArray Resizing arrays

Resizing for arrays behaves the same way as for matrices, the two being cousins who differ in their manners at table but not in the law of removals.
You provide the new number of rows and columns as arguments to \c resize().

\code
ArrayXXf a(2,2);
a << 1, 2, 3, 4;
a.resize(3,3);
// a is now 3x3 and the values are uninitialized.
\endcode

\section TopicResizing_ResizeLike Resizing to match the Dimensions of a Neighbor, with \link PlainObjectBase::resizeLike() resizeLike() \endlink

You can resize a matrix or vector to match the dimensions of another object using \link PlainObjectBase::resizeLike() resizeLike(eigenBase) \endlink, in the spirit of a gentleman who, having seen his neighbor's drawing-room, determines to have one precisely as large.
This method is also **destructive** (data is lost); the emulation of a neighbor is not to be had without some sacrifice of the original.

\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
MatrixXd n(4,4);
m.resizeLike(n); 
// m is now 4x4.
\endcode


<b>Note on Vectors:</b> When applied to vectors, \c resizeLike() matches the **size** (number of coefficients) of the other object, 
but maintains the row/column orientation of the vector being resized; the vector will copy his neighbor's length, but on no account his posture.

\code
RowVectorXd r(2);
VectorXd c(5);

// r is resized to be a row-vector of size 5 (1x5), matching c's size.
// It does NOT become a column-vector.
r.resizeLike(c); 
\endcode

\section TopicResizing_Conservative Resizing with a Tender Regard for the Existing Values, by Means of \link PlainObjectBase::conservativeResize(Index,Index) conservativeResize() \endlink

If you need to resize a matrix while keeping its current values, 
use \link PlainObjectBase::conservativeResize(Index,Index) conservativeResize(rows, cols) \endlink, the removal-man of the more careful sort, who wraps every cup in paper.

\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
m << 1, 2, 3, 4;
m.conservativeResize(3,3);
// m is now:
// 1 2 ?
// 3 4 ?
// ? ? ?
// The '?' are uninitialized values.
\endcode


When using \c conservativeResize():
- **Preservation:** The existing values are preserved, and arrive at the new address in the same condition as they left the old.
- **Alignment:** The matrix is resized relative to the **top-left** corner, that being the corner at which the whole establishment is fastened down, and from which it grows or shrinks as a tree from its root.
- **New Data:** Any newly allocated coefficients (if the matrix grows) are **uninitialized**. You should fill them before use, the new rooms being, to say the least of it, unfurnished.

Just like \c resize(), you can resize **vectors** and **arrays** and keep previous values, using \c conservativeResize().

And just like \c resize(), you can use \c Eigen::NoChange to resize only one dimension conservatively:
\code
MatrixXd m(2,2);
m << 1, 2, 3, 4;

// Add a new row (now 3x2), keeping existing values
m.conservativeResize(3, Eigen::NoChange); 
\endcode

\section TopicResizing_Assignment Automatic resizing on assignment, or the Accommodating Disposition of the Left-Hand Side

By default, when you assign one matrix to another, Eigen automatically resizes the left-hand side to match the size of the right-hand side; the left-hand side, like a hospitable host, making room without a murmur for whatever guest the right-hand side may bring.

\code
MatrixXf a(2,2);
MatrixXf b(4,4);
a = b; // a is now 4x4
\endcode

\subsection TopicResizing_DisableAuto Disabling automatic resizing, for the Host who Would Rather Be Consulted

In some applications, you may want to prevent automatic resizing to avoid unexpected memory allocations, which are to the performance-minded programmer what unexpected relations are to the man of limited means. 
You can disable this behavior by defining the \c EIGEN_NO_AUTOMATIC_RESIZING preprocessor macro.

If this macro is defined, the assignment `a = b` will trigger an assertion failure at runtime if the dimensions of `a` and `b` do not match; the beadle, in short, will turn the guest from the door.

\code
#define EIGEN_NO_AUTOMATIC_RESIZING
#include <Eigen/Dense>

void function() {
  MatrixXf a(2,2);
  MatrixXf b(4,4);
  a = b; // ERROR: Runtime assertion failure
}
\endcode

\section TopicResizing_Fixed Fixed-size matrices, who Are Not to Be Moved

Resizing methods are technically available on fixed-size matrices for API uniformity, but they will trigger an assertion failure if you try to actually change the dimensions; the method is present, as the knocker is present upon the door of a house in which nobody will ever be at home to the visitor. 
Because the dimensions of a fixed-size matrix (like \c Matrix4f) are determined at compile-time, they cannot be changed at runtime.

\code
Matrix4f m;
m.resize(4,4); // Legal, no-op
m.resize(5,5); // ERROR: Runtime assertion failure
\endcode

*/
}
